      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "To examine the changing relationship between general and vascular surgical workload on a vascular \"firm\", over a 6-year period."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Retrospective review."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Leicester Royal Infirmary and Professorial Surgical Unit, U.K. 1987-1992."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Analysis of audit of all surgical admissions. Relation of vascular surgery to general surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There has been a slight decrease (5%) in the number of general surgical elective admissions. Overall, the number of general surgical admissions, both elective and emergency, show a slight increase of about 3%. In contrast the number of vascular admissions increased by 42%. Of the general surgical procedures carried out 75.9% were either minor or intermediate, whereas 92.5% of vascular procedures were coded as major or higher. There has been a three times increase in the number of carotid endarterectomies, a similar increase in the number of the femorodistal bypass grafts and a halving of the number of major amputations. There has also been a five times increase in the number of angioplasties carried out."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our figures show the progressive, rapid increase in vascular surgical workload, compared to general surgery, and the need for the continued expansion of vascular surgery as a speciality."
        }
      ]},
